WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:14.580
I warmly welcome you to Friedrich Nietzsche,
 one of the great European

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:21.780
philosophers. Today we are dealing 
with the famous "will to power" and Nietzsche’s

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:29.400
great vision of the overman. And you will see
 that Nietzsche has numerous ideas that

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:39.780
are of burning topicality. And already in 1882
he formulated a small sentence

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:46.620
which has acquired great fame. 
It's very short and I’ve brought it with me 

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:53.377
For you: "God is dead". 
This little phrase is known everywhere nowadays,

00:00:53.377 --> 00:00:58.800
not just in Europe, but also in America. "God is dead!".
Everyone thinks ‘I've heard this phrase before’.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.090
Very few people know that this sentence
came from Nietzsche, but

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:08.580
it is well known. It spread around the world
like wildfire because Nietzsche 

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:14.220
in saying this, brought something to expression
that was to become a big problem

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.500
in the next few centuries for the 
entire world, or at least the western world.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:26.910
By saying "God is dead" Nietzsche does not mean
just any event, i.e. that God died suddenly.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:35.130
He is speaking of a process. He says: "What I’m telling
is the story of the next two centuries.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:42.900
I am describing what is to come, what has to come: 
namely, the advent of nihilism."

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.750
So what he says – "God is dead" – means:
This is about the history of the next two

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:54.990
centuries. He said that around 1880. If you
 extrapolate 1980, 2080, that's a process that's

00:01:54.990 --> 00:02:02.400
extends to our own times and it's the process
of the rise of nihilism and mass atheism.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:09.330
And if we're honest, up to half a million people
 are actually leaving the churches every year.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:16.380
Even in this year of Luther’s anniversary
Many people have left and one has to assume

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:24.390
that in just a few years the vast majority of the
 European population will no longer be in the

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:31.200
church. From that point of view, Nietzsche’s prediction 
was of course quite accurate. Part of the reason why

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.960
this is happening, he says, is of course
 the emerging natural sciences. You have to

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.620
remember that Nietzsche's contemporary was
Charles Darwin, who was the first to say that we are

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:50.760
not all descended from Adam and Eve,
 but rather we are higher mammals that have

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.790
slowly emerged through evolution via the apes, 
via the hominids. But it wasn't just Darwin. Numerous

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.310
scientists have shaken and shattered the old
religious worldview in which we felt safe and secure 

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:10.230
for thousands of years. It was a time of enormous
disruption: in medicine, in physics,

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:16.170
in all areas. To take just medicine:
 It used to be thought that the plague, cholera,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.910
these serious diseases, especially the plague,
were the work of the devil, or rather

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:29.640
ia punishment from God. But even before 1900,
 a Russian scientist said: "No, it's

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.970
just a bacterial infection" and everything
was put to the test. At that time there was a

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:45.450
big breakthrough. More and more doubts were raised, 
not only about Adam and Eve, about the immaculate

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:50.370
conception, about the whole story of creation - but
 in the end also about God himself.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:59.190
And now Nietzsche says: "Where has God gone?" 
And then something exciting happens. He says:

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:07.260
 "We killed him, you and I! We are all his killers!"
 And what he means by that is very simple: it wasn't

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:14.010
 just Darwin and the natural scientists
 who killed God with their new theories explaining

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:21.000
the world. No, we all did, because we withdrew
 the power that gives God existence,

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.730
because we no longer believed in him in that way.
 Nietzsche was the first post-modern

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:32.770
thinker. He even wrote a book
called The Antichrist. He was an atheist,

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:39.820
a committed atheist, but he was also the first
 post-modern thinker. Why post-modern?

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:45.910
What does post-modern mean? What does modern mean?
By modernity we generally understand

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:51.370
this great awakening of the Enlightenment,
 when the natural sciences began, when

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:58.510
the French Enlighteners – Rousseau, Voltaire, Montesquieu –
 suddenly demanded the separation of powers, asked

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:02.890
"Who shall rule the people if not the people?" and so on.
 That was a great awakening,

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:09.850
the Enlightenment – or, as the French say, 
“illumination” – 

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:16.240
means that the light of reason
 should go everywhere and should

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.580
illuminate and dispel, so to speak, superstition, belief,
 myth and ancient worldviews. That was the modern age.

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:27.610
Nietzsche is the first post-modern thinker.
 Saying that is no longer enough for him. The

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:33.550
Enlightenment was also very critical. Kant, Locke, Hume
 and all the Enlightenment thinkers were

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:38.860
also critical of religion, but it is not enough
 for Nietzsche to criticize that. He says:

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.390
the real question that Man will have
 to ask himself in the next few centuries

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:49.390
is a completely different one. What happens
 when the last human has stopped

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:59.140
believing in God? What happens then?
 That interested him. And then he says - quote:

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:05.080
"Since the belief has ceased that a god guides the fate
 of the world on a large scale,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:11.500
people themselves have to set goals
 that encompass the whole earth.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.300
Herein lies the tremendous task." 
So if we are no longer secure

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.870
in the religious worldview that life is a task that

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:24.640
we have to fulfill in order to get to paradise,
 if that no longer supports us, then

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:31.450
we have to take on this task ourselves.
Humans themselves must now

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:38.890
give themselves global goals that span the entire world
 in order to shape the future. And now, he says - and

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:42.670
that's why he's considered a post-modern thinker - we
 have a big problem. He says that

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.710
people aren't ready for it. Because, 
his contemporaries, he says,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:53.800
when he looks at them, all he can do is
 shake his head. As soon

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:01.120
as God is dead, they fall into belief in false idols
 and new idols are erected. One of those idols,

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.670
he says, is nationalism and the form of this that
 especially concerned him: the German one. It started then,

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:11.200
you know, the founding of the Reich in 1870 and everything.
 The German nationalism and supremacism of that time

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.780
got on his nerves. If there is any nation, he said, 
that can be proud of what they are,

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:23.500
patriotic about their culture can be proud of,
 then ist is the French. And then he says here,

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:31.600
"Is there any thought behind this herd nationalism? What
 value could there be in stirring up this crude selfishness

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.410
now that everything points to
 greater and common interests?"

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:41.560
 And it always annoyed him, these fraternities,

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:45.760
the student movements, these marching singers,
 the fuss with the "Kaiser",

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:49.990
it got on his nerves. Because he says
 it's just a new idol. God is dead,

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:54.430
we no longer have meaning through religion. 
So now we try to create the meaning we lack 

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:58.750
by saying “we're German” or “we're French” or

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:04.120
“we’re English”. And that's wrong. The next big idol,
 he says, and this is even worse,

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:08.635
is that people now derive their self-worth, 
after God is dead, from

00:08:08.635 --> 00:08:16.660
 being higher than others and thereby
feel, even within the age of nihilism,

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:22.780
a little more important than others. And the 
racists are always looking for some group.

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:28.660
For example, the anti-Semites. And then Nietzsche says: 
"The anti-Semites cannot forgive the Jews for the fact that 

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:35.523
the Jews have brains - and money: anti-Semitism - 
a name for those who have ‘come off badly’.

00:08:35.523 --> 00:08:40.975
There is no more brazen and stupid gang in Germany
 than these anti-Semites."

00:08:40.975 --> 00:08:48.085
I mean, Nietzsche scolded many things,
 but he despised anti-Semites in particular. He

00:08:48.085 --> 00:08:53.140
was friends with Wagner for a while
 before they naturally fell out. And

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:59.170
there he also writes: "Lately Wagner has been
 unbearable. This constant anti-Semitism,

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:05.920
he's almost as bad as my sister." Because 
that was deeply repugnant to him. Because he

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.880
said "God is dead – so now we have to derive
 our self-worth from something else and it can’t be

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:15.790
that you derive self-worth from something like that.
 As you can see, Nietzsche was anything but

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:22.090
a Nazi. Any appropriation in this direction
 is not possible in view of these statements

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:27.970
by Nietzsche. The only thing that Hitler really had
 from Nietzsche and could copy one to one

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:33.040
was Nietzsche's original walking stick.
 After Nietzsche

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.890
was already dead, Nietzsche's sister gave it
 to him, gave Hitler this walking stick,

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:42.100
but Hitler almost certainly never read Nietzsche.
Then Nietzsche goes on to say:

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:47.410
Another great idol is also there now, which may

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:52.390
play a role for centuries to come. He was incredibly
 farsighted politically and he says: It's socialism.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:10:00.250
Socialism. - You know, 1848, Karl Marx,
 Communist Manifesto, he of course

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:04.750
noticed this movement, although 
there was not yet any existing socialist state,

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:10.750
but his prognosis was already negative at that time.
 He says: "Socialism covets an abundance

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:16.960
of state power such as only despotism has ever had.

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:24.370
Yes, it surpasses everything that has come before it
 by striving for the formal annihilation of the individual,

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:29.770
and it drives the word justice like a nail into the head

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:34.840
of the half-educated masses to rob them completely
 of their minds." So he also criticized socialism as a new

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:41.440
idol, because he says that the socialists are striving
 for despotic state power,

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.910
in which only one party gets to govern and 
the party is always right and

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:49.810
all individuals are brought into line. 
And then he says that socialism cannot work

00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:55.510
because people are not equal.
 We are not all the same.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:59.440
Individualism was very important 
to Nietzsche. And he says that if we

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.570
give everyone the same amount of money, if everyone
is co-ordinated and regimented, that can't go well.

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:09.670
This abolition of the individual will take revenge.
 That is why socialism is also a

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:14.110
new religion for him. He says well,
 God is dead, now people don't believe

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:17.860
they're going to paradise. So what happens?
 They are promised a

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:23.020
workers' paradise and a worldly promise of salvation
 is given instead of the metaphysical promise of

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:26.590
salvation. But that doesn't work either. 
And he says it's also bad

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:29.770
to hold on to these idols. 
And the fourth great idol,

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.710
ladies and gentlemen, what else will that be? It is

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:39.100
the one we believe in today. 
Yes, capitalism. Quote: "The commercial man

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:45.820
examines all that is created from the viewpoint
of supply and demand, in order to set

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:51.880
a thing’s worth and value for himself. This, 
made a characteristic of an entire culture,

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:56.650
is what you people of the
 next century will be proud of. "

00:11:56.650 --> 00:12:00.820
This is remarkable, because at that time the
 beginnings of globalization and capitalism were

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:05.650
already there, but not yet fully developed.
 But he says that the “commercial man”,

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:11.110
the businessman or the capitalist or entrepreneur,
 measures everything only according to

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:16.450
the value of the goods on the market according to supply
 and demand. He rents out an apartment

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:21.670
at the highest price at which he can 
 still finds tenants for it. And

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.380
everything is determined only by supply and demand.
 There are no other principles

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:30.220
of justice any more. And everyone lives by this principle.
 And there is a

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.400
new idolatry. People will only measure
 themselves according to

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:39.430
how far they’ve “made it”, how rich they are,
 and so on, and worship filthy Mammon

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:43.720
i.e. money. And this is just as much a god
or idol worship as socialism and

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:49.600
nationalism. And these idols have a chance,
 says Nietzsche, because they comfort us,

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.620
because it is not easy to endure nihilism,
 the cold breath

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.350
of nihilism, after God is dead.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:58.600
So we console ourselves with these idols.
And now he asks the question:

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:04.060
How? "How do we console ourselves, 
we, the murderers of all murderers?

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:08.560
Shouldn't we become gods ourselves?"
 That is now

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.490
the central question. This is a 
post-modern question. How

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:15.720
are we to go on, now that God
 has lost his power to create

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:22.290
being, and now that ever fewer people go to church
 and get their whole world view from it? What’s

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:27.450
next? How do we console each other, 
the murderers of all murderers? Shouldn't we

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:31.560
become gods ourselves? And here
 his answer is a resounding "yes".

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:39.450
We must now take the fate of this world
 into our own hands. We must now

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:45.660
take on this great task. The only problem is - 
and this is why we’ve repeatedly mentioned the “idols” -

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:50.280
we're not even able to do it yet. Not yet, anyway.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:55.380
We don't yet have the strength to take
on this task. But we must get there

00:13:55.380 --> 00:14:02.160
because: "All gods are dead, now we want
 the overman to live." And that's

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:07.290
what we're going to deal with now. What is this
 overman? Where should we go after Nietzsche?

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:11.550
But you see how up-to-date this actually is.
He's already very close to us and that's

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:17.580
why he's the postmodern thinker. 
Well - already as a 25-year-old,

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:22.800
as a young guy, Nietzsche found the key
 to understanding the whole world, or

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:28.770
he believed that he’d discovered it. Do you know where? 
Where did he find that key? Ironically,

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:34.740
in ancient Greek tragedy. He says
these old things, these old Greek tragedies,

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:40.860
recognized what it is to be human and 
what the whole world is all about. Because

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:45.510
in these old tragedies there are always
 only two major principles: the hero

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:50.400
who fights and acts and is always torn
 between the Dionysian and

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:57.270
the Apollonian principle. So I have another quote
 with me. He says: "There are two states in

00:14:57.270 --> 00:15:03.540
which art, i.e. the theater itself, appears like a force
 of nature in people: on the one hand as a compulsion

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:11.310
to have a vision, on the other hand as a compulsion to
engage in an orgy." Nietzsche says that these two

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:18.250
principles exist in each of us - not only in the hero
 of ancient Greece. 

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:25.540
I'll explain this briefly. One is the Apollonian
 principle. This is what he calls the

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:32.230
compulsion to have a vision. So, we want to have
 visions. We want to plan the world. We want to

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:37.000
shape the world. He names this principle after 
the god Apollo

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:45.130
Apollo, a Greek god, was the god 
of future planning, also of prophecy,

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:51.070
the god of reason. He was the radiant 
god and the founder of cities. In a word, he was

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:57.070
a doer, we would say today, but also a thinker
 who planned and structured. So the

00:15:57.070 --> 00:16:03.250
Apollonian principle stands for the rational,
 Planning element in Man. But also there

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:10.690
is Dionysus. Everyone probably knows 
the good god of drunkenness, i.e. the god

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:16.030
of wine, not of drunkenness – of wine, yes, and
 of drunkenness only if you overdo it. And

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:26.980
of course Dionysus also stands for creativity,
 for the chaotic, for the purely emotional,

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:31.060
for passion, for passionate knowledge.
 In the pictures of him he is always

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:37.660
with women and wine. And these two principles fight
 against each other in every human being - even

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:43.570
in classical tragedy. So it's often the case 
that we plan and do something and at the

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:49.000
last moment we do it differently for 
some reason. We know what is reasonable

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.900
and that what we are doing now is unreasonable
but we do it anyway, and so you have to

00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:59.830
imagine that the Apollonian always tries
 to shape the world first and then

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:06.670
the Dionysian makes everything dance again.
An example is 'Oedipus' by Sophocles, a very

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:12.670
old tragedy. Oedipus always tries to do 
everything right. He arrives in a town

00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:17.560
where there is a despotic king and he 
fights him, kills him, falls in love with

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:22.240
the queen, marries her – and at some point realizes
 that he has married his own mother

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:25.870
He didn't even know that because he was
 abandoned as a child – and then of course

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:31.390
everything is in chaos again. That's how you 
have to imagine it. And Nietzsche says, that's

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:35.560
life. The Greeks realized how our life 
really is. And we need 'amor fati',

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:41.500
the love of fate and that means we never
 get out of suffering completely. But

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:47.320
we have to accept these two
 principles, that's the way it is. But

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:51.700
a big mistake was already made in ancient times.
 With Euripides, who was also a great tragic author,

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:58.180
the Dionysian element suddenly disappeared, 
was suppressed, no longer really respected.

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:03.490
Because in classical tragedy there was always a chorus,
 the lamentation chorus. This chorus always represented

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:07.150
feelings and the Dionysian. And 
what was on the stage was more

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:12.370
Apollonian. And at the latest under Euripides
 the chorus was omitted. The Dionysian played

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:17.020
less and less of a role. The rational man became
 stronger and stronger in antiquity and

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:24.550
this went even further with Plato and his main 
character Socrates. All of a sudden the view was that

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:29.200
the Dionysian is bad and evil and that we have to turn 
ourselves upwards, toward the idea of justice,

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:38.320
of truth, of beauty. And then suddenly being human
 was completely redefined. "Happiness", says

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:44.650
Nietzsche, "just means you have
 to imitate Socrates and create a

00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:51.070
daylight against dark desires, the daylight
 of reason. Every yielding to the instincts,

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:56.140
to the unconscious, leads us down." And
 then, Nietzsche says, something happens:

00:18:56.140 --> 00:19:01.630
a mind-body separation. The physical,
 the Dionysian, our lust, our sensuality

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:06.670
become evil. That leads down. The spirit, the reason,
 the intellect, that leads us upward.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:15.880
And that was a fatefully bad development,
 because, as he then puts it:

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:21.850
"The moralism of the Greek philosophers 
from Plato onwards is pathological."

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:27.340
This trend becomes even more sick and pathological with the
 emergence of Christianity because Christianity is still

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:32.680
more hostile to the body. I don't know how many of
you were raised Catholic.

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:38.800
We're in Bavaria. I learned during puberty that
 you shouldn't think unchastely,

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:45.700
which of course no pubescent person can hep but do. And Nietzsche says that with

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:52.150
Christianity this developed in a fatal way. Because
in ancient times there was “the wise man and

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:56.410
the fool”. And the wise man developed 
the soul upwards and the fool just

00:19:56.410 --> 00:20:01.060
followed his desires. Plato says that the latter is
like the existence of a duck. One just eats and excretes

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:05.620
and enjoys it. But in Christianity it gets much
worse. Because there, there is a bipolarity.

00:20:05.620 --> 00:20:10.630
The bipolarity in Christianity is between good and evil
 and one can fall entirely under the sway of evil and the Devil

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:15.460
or one can turn oneself toward God. And the 
body, bodily nature, temptation, these are 

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:22.600
for the Christian just secondary things. And Nietzsche
criticizes Christianity on four grounds. First:

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:27.700
"I condemn Christianity, I raise against the 
Christian church the most terrible

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:38.260
of all accusations that an accuser has ever uttered.
 It has made a worthlessness out of every value,

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:46.150
a lie out of every truth." In his remarks 
he also explains that in Christianity’s main symbol,

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:51.940
this God on the cross, that is, this Jesus, the 
suffering one, everything has been lost that

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:55.240
is actually important for us
 humans, namely courage,

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:59.740
heroic struggles, standing up for oneself. This
 "turn the other cheek",

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:06.430
he says, that was a mistake, that was a
 mistake. Secondly, he says that the pity

00:21:06.430 --> 00:21:11.980
that Christianity propagates, so to speak, 
is not good either. "Pity," he says,

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:17.200
"is a waste of feelings, a parasite
 harmful to moral health,

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:22.630
it cannot possibly be a duty to increase the 
evils in the world". For very often, he says,

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:27.250
out of pity we give alms and other things besides 
and thereby prolong the suffering of people

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:31.690
who might otherwise rebel and do something
 that would change society. But no,

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:37.030
we all support this compassion which does
not solve any problems. And compassion,

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:41.410
he says, can also be a sign of contempt
which is implied in someone’s feeling sorry for you.

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:44.290
So pity is, for Nietzsche, something very bad.
 Incidentally, he also says: 

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:49.300
"Beggars should be abolished, because you
 always feel bad when you walk past a 

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:52.840
beggar. Either you give him something,
then you feel bad, or you

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:54.490
don't give him anything, then you also feel bad”.

00:21:54.490 --> 00:22:00.880
So definitely, pity, that wasn't for him. His third
major criticism of Christianity was the following:

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:07.150
It gives rise to an entirely new type of human being.
 He says: "Perhaps up to now there has been no

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:15.610
more dangerous ideology, no greater nonsense
 in psychological matters, than this will to the good:

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:20.470
it has given rise to the most disgusting type,
 the most unfree human being, the “yes-man”,

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:27.010
people have been taught that it’s only as “yes-men” that
they’re on the right path to divinity." You know that

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:32.650
humility is very important in Christianity and that
 one is constantly committed to doing good,

00:22:32.650 --> 00:22:36.430
because life is a test, a task. And he says that

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:41.710
in the end there arise thereby “yes-men”, hypocrites,
 and this doesn't only apply to Christianity.

00:22:42.430 --> 00:22:48.700
You know, regardless of whether it's in Islam,
 there's prayer and bowing down to the ground

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:55.060
or the Christians who kneel down, he says, 
there results a certain kind of person.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:23:01.780
He doesn't think that's healthy. And the last
 thing he criticizes is the sentence: "If

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:07.360
thine eye offends thee, pluck it out”.
The Christian who follows that advice

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:14.080
and thinks he has killed his sensuality
 is wrong. This sensuality lives on in an uncanny

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:20.470
vampiric way and torments him in 
disgusting disguises." So Nietzsche says that

00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:25.660
we can’t just live in a purely Apollonian way
 and completely suppress the Dionysian.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:31.630
Here, by the way, he anticipates
 something that Sigmund Freud later

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:39.010
said. Fundamentally, psychology as a whole, but
Freud’s psychoanalysis in particular: The desires,

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:44.050
our desires, our instincts and also the
 great desires in life that we suppress

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:50.500
are not simply gone, they smolder 
and can even turn against us.

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:57.250
So numerous neurotic illnesses are
attributed to the fact that people had to

00:23:57.250 --> 00:24:04.000
suppress too many of their longings, lusts and 
desires in life for too long. And he says that

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:08.920
that is also what's dangerous about this 
Christian ideology, as he calls it.

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:13.840
All in all, this great hatred of the body 
bothers him, because, as we have seen,

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:19.540
he saw from early on that everyone
 is also a Dionysian and that's why

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:26.020
he says: "I am body completely and nothing else." 
“Body am I”, with this

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:31.540
he contradicts the hostility to the body of the entire tradition,
 including that of philosophy. This tradition says

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:37.240
we are primarily rational beings, animal rationale,
 and that the body is the servant of the spirit,

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:41.380
which only carries out what the spirit decides. But it is
 not like that. Nietzsche, by contrast, speaks

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:46.210
of the “great reason” of the body because he
 says it would also be a complete illusion

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:53.830
to believe that our reason governs us,
our consciousness. Because - by the

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:58.900
way, today's scientists would confirm this - there has
 been life for an incredibly long time. Scientifically

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:02.410
we would have to say, life has existed
 for about three billion years -

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:09.610
three billion! - hardly imaginable. And 
also the hominids, Nietzsche speaks of

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:14.260
the semi-animals, they've been around
 forever. But they were in the woods in

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:20.260
hordes as hunters and gatherers. They weren't
 conscious either. And only at the very end of

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:25.810
the history of our evolution did consciousness
 arise at all. But we

00:25:25.810 --> 00:25:32.470
still have that legacy within us. And that's why
 it can't be taken away. He then also says:

00:25:32.470 --> 00:25:40.360
"Once you were monkeys and even now Man
 is more monkey than any monkey." That's

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:46.000
  the tendency he has there. 
But given that we lived

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:52.030
for billions of years only instinctively,
 according to stimulus and reaction and desire,

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:56.800
including as protozoa and during the whole of
 evolution, how did it come about

00:25:56.800 --> 00:26:01.330
that today we have a consciousness 
at all? - And even worse, a guilty

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:07.720
conscience? And then he wrote this famous
 chapter in the 'Genealogy of Morals' about

00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:12.760
the emergence of bad conscience.
 And he says that

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:20.330
the whole madness came about at the
moment when people left the forests and

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:25.730
founded the first communities, villages,
 kraals and then also states. Because

00:26:25.730 --> 00:26:31.820
something bad happened within these
 communities and states. There were

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:36.170
laws and rules. And now nobody was
 allowed to harm anyone anymore.

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:40.670
But you had to suppress all the instincts

00:26:40.670 --> 00:26:45.890
you had before, the anger, the attention, the aggression,

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:51.500
the running away. You know, in the forest, you
could still hear every cracking sound, you could

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:55.940
smell it, you knew “now I have to run away” if 
you were threatened or if you were attacked while

00:26:55.940 --> 00:27:01.040
hunting. We were sure in our instincts.
Nietzsche says we've now lost all these instincts.

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:06.680
The whole development of evolution has been negative.
Water used to carry us when we were still

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:11.600
in the water. Now our feet have to carry us.
 And it was also a very negative step

00:27:11.600 --> 00:27:17.870
when suddenly self-awareness arose through 
the founding of the state. Because, he says:

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:24.830
"Suddenly all human beings’ instincts were invalidated
 and hung up. I don't think there was ever

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:33.230
such a feeling of misery on earth" as
 when suddenly the instincts were devalued.

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:37.880
And you know it is actually like this: 
the instincts are devalued - and not

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:42.500
only devalued, they are taboo. So if we

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:45.890
get a choleric attack at work and 
shout out loud because we're angry,

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:51.980
then everyone says: “But you're aggressive”.
 Sure, says Nietzsche, because

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:57.740
the instinct is still there, but it can no longer
be lived out. These instincts have been hung up

00:27:57.740 --> 00:28:05.600
and devalued. And now in the state, when
 we live together in such large groups,

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:09.980
it is about something else: these
 people, that is us, are

00:28:09.980 --> 00:28:16.100
reduced to thinking, reasoning,
 calculating, combining causes and effects,

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:24.680
these unfortunates: reduced to their consciousness,
 to their poorest and most misguided

00:28:24.680 --> 00:28:29.420
organ!" So all of a sudden we had
 to rely on our poorest organ,

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:31.640
on our consciousness, and we thought,

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:37.580
and calculated whereas we’d been used
for many years, to being completely instinctive

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:41.255
throughout all of evolution . Now we 
have this loss of instinct. At that

00:28:41.255 --> 00:28:45.140
moment -- what happened? Then
 he says: "All instincts that

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:48.860
are not discharged outwards turn

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:52.550
inwards". Now it's getting exciting.
 Now he says: "Because we can't

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:54.680
for example, take food when we’re hungry” –
 Small children still do it in the

00:28:54.680 --> 00:28:59.720
shop. If you don't guard the shopping 
trolley, they reach over and

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:03.440
get the chocolate, open it and eat it,
 because they have instinctive confidence.

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:07.340
get the chocolate, open it and eat. Of course,
 because they have instinctive confidence.

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:13.370
Later, of course, they have to learn that 
things don’t work that way –.”then we

00:29:13.370 --> 00:29:20.780
turn all our instincts inwards". Aggression too.
 Aggression saved us for thousands of years

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:25.850
because we defended territories, conquered new
 ones, because we fought against other hordes

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:30.530
but now we have to suppress this energy
of aggression

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:37.310
which is directed inwards now and leads
 to self-punishment impulses. And that is

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:43.910
the origin of the bad conscience. So he 
says: "The enmity t, the cruelty,

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:48.650
the desire for persecution, for change,
 for destruction - all this directed against

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:54.470
the owner of such instincts: That is the
 origin of the "bad conscience". The

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:58.400
penalties, he says, do the rest, insofar as we
 know, of course, that if we don't follow the

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:03.410
rules of the state we will be punished. But 
ultimately bad conscience is also

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:07.790
fed by our own energy, that we know
 we shouldn't do that now and

00:30:07.790 --> 00:30:13.400
keep ourselves in check with our own 
energy. And the bottom line is: Both

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:19.460
consciousness and conscience are very
 late phenomena in evolution, which

00:30:19.460 --> 00:30:24.740
didn't exist at all for a long time and which ultimately
shouldn't play the decisive role in our lives either.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:32.370
Rather, the decisive thing is,
says Nietzsche, that we still have

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:37.380
an enormous will - and we feel that in ourselves.
 We have desires, we have longings, we have

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:42.270
needs and he doesn't just mean eating
and drinking. He says that all the needs,

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:46.680
longings and desires that we have result in our
wanting. Every human being has such a

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:56.790
will. And now we are already at the heart of the matter: 
The will to power. Then he says: "This world: a

00:30:56.790 --> 00:31:02.790
monster without beginning, without end, is the
 will to power - and nothing more! And you

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:10.140
yourselves are also this will to power - and
nothing more!" How can he claim that this world,

00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:15.210
a monster of power, is the will to power?
Is the world really the will to power?

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:20.220
To this he says: Yes, the whole world is the
 will to power, and that is because:

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:27.030
Every plant grows according to the sun,
Extends itself, partly putting other plants in the shade,

00:31:27.030 --> 00:31:30.060
which then extend themselves too 
and do the same thing. But if

00:31:30.060 --> 00:31:33.930
there is a little tree and a big one, the little one
usually dies or the one that grows faster

00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:40.470
stays. So there is also an unconditional
 will to live, to survive and to improve

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:45.180
oneself. Also in plants and even in chemistry.
 He says when substances mix,

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:49.830
You remember that from chemistry class, then
 The resulting substances change colour, they react

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:54.630
with each other. Either they mix or they
 repel each other. But in any case,

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:59.010
says Nietzsche, forces interact. Forces are
 at work in all of nature. That is why he says:

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:03.840
the whole of nature is a monster of power.
About the planets too he writes a nice sentence:

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:11.490
The supposed laws of nature are nothing
 more than the balance of power. Well,

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:16.710
the fact that the planets attract each other and
 have these orbits, that's calculated exactly

00:32:16.710 --> 00:32:20.460
how much attraction one has on 
the other, and that's how it levels

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:24.360
out. It's about power and forces –
and the same goes for people. 

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:28.410
an interesting example of the first 
living beings, by the way. At that

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:31.710
time there were already microscopes 
and such techniques. They were

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:34.920
able to magnify things optically. 
And then he says: The amoeba,

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:41.400
the smallest little animal, also has tiny hairs and wafts
 around. As soon as the amoeba encounters

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:47.180
something organic, it encloses it. 
And if ist likes it, then that gets

00:32:47.180 --> 00:32:53.420
integrated into the amoeba, it eats it up, so 
to speak. And this appropriation we have had in

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:58.580
nature since the beginning. So, the will to power and also
we ourselves as human beings, we are nothing other than

00:32:58.580 --> 00:33:07.430
this will to power. And that's why he also says: "Everything
 that happens can be reduced to the intention

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:16.610
of increasing power." But that's how it works even
 now, he says, that we always affect others.

00:33:16.610 --> 00:33:25.100
Increasing our power can also mean

00:33:25.100 --> 00:33:32.420
hindering others in their own self-improvement. But there's no getting
around it. Therefore, he says: "Life always lives at the expense of

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:37.850
other life. Anyone who does not 
understand this has not yet

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:44.780
taken the first step towards honesty." He says 
we can't help it. For example, if you apply for

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:49.820
a job as a head of a department and get it, 
then x colleagues have applied, they

00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:55.250
won't get it and then they're sad. So, every success
comes at the expense of the failure

00:33:55.250 --> 00:34:01.820
of others. Or the Olympic champion, who then
 stands on the podium, with the wreath,

00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:08.270
shining golden in the glory of victory, stands up there
 because at the same time he darkens and shadows others

00:34:08.270 --> 00:34:14.630
who are not up there. Nietzsche says we can't live
 at all without affecting others. That's

00:34:14.630 --> 00:34:21.530
not possible. And then he also has more subtle examples
 that I find very exciting. For example, he says

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:27.020
that if two men fall in love with the same woman
 or even three men,

00:34:27.020 --> 00:34:33.890
and one gets her, then two others
 will be left behind. Or one of his

00:34:33.890 --> 00:34:40.400
most subtle examples, he was also a great 
psychologist: mothers. An early childhood

00:34:40.400 --> 00:34:44.570
phase is something interesting for him, because
 he says mothers who, shall we say,

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:50.820
love one child more than the other or the
others, back then everyone had five or six,

00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:54.030
they don't do it consciously. They are fair. They give

00:34:54.030 --> 00:34:58.170
everyone the same amount. But
 they can't help it, one being is

00:34:58.170 --> 00:35:04.020
somehow closer to them. And the worst thing, he
 says, is that the children sense it and that's why

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:06.180
it's not the mother's fault
for what she's doing. He says:

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:10.350
you finally have to think beyond 
good and evil, which is the title of one of

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:13.260
his books: Beyond Good and Evil.
 The mother who

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:16.470
just loves, subconsciously, one child a bit
more than another

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:18.480
it's not her fault  – not really – 

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:21.090
if she always tries to be fair and just.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:24.180
But the less loved child may

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:26.910
feel neglected and has problems dealing with it.

00:35:26.910 --> 00:35:32.915
And that's why we can't avoid it: Life always 
lives at the expense of other life, 

00:35:32.915 --> 00:35:36.615
That's how it is with us. 
Another nice sentence from him is:

00:35:36.615 --> 00:35:40.980
"The insects do not sting out of
 malice, but because they also

00:35:40.980 --> 00:35:45.197
want to live." So he means the 
mosquitoes. And it's correct.

00:35:45.197 --> 00:35:49.860
You always hear: Bssss! In reality,
 what are they supposed to

00:35:49.860 --> 00:35:53.970
do? Even if for us it's terror,
 which is malicious, they

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:59.250
can't help it. And we too often cannot do otherwise.
 And no one goes through life without

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:07.140
causing problems for others at some point. And because
 that's the way it is, he says, because we're there

00:36:07.140 --> 00:36:13.830
he says: "I judge the power of a will
 by how much resistance,

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:17.280
pain, torture it endures and how 
it manages to turn these things to its advantage."

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:20.070
He says we all have obstacles in life.

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:23.310
But we can use resistance as an opportunity

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:27.540
to grow and turn it to our advantage.

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:32.280
So our self-development, our will to 
power, proceeds against resistance,

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:37.740
always in life. But he just values ​​an individual
 according to whether the individual manages

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:43.710
to overcome the things that are against
 him and to make them into his will. Now,

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:49.080
of course, one can say, and Nietzsche was also heavily
 criticized for this, that there are no limits to this!

00:36:49.080 --> 00:36:52.290
If everyone 
Lives out their will to power,

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:59.490
then at some point enormous egoism will
 emerge. Everyone develops their will to

00:36:59.490 --> 00:37:03.330
power. What limits should it have
 if not even pity is a limit,

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:11.370
if not even pity limits our egoism?
Where is the limit of the will to power?

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:17.430
Can you overpower others? Are you allowed to
 kill others for the sake of your development and

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:24.420
your own higher goal? And 
there he says. It can actually

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:32.940
be so. That’s quite radical - a radical thought
 of Nietzsche’s. He says: It may be,

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:38.880
it may be, it is possible for each of us, "That something
 is a hundred times more important than

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:45.120
the question of whether we are well or
 badly off and consequently whether others

00:37:45.120 --> 00:37:52.080
are well or badly off. In short; it may be that we have an end 
for which no one hesitates to make human sacrifices."

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:58.200
So, it can actually be the case that, in in order to develop our highest goal

00:37:58.200 --> 00:38:03.600
and our will to power, we should not consider
 ourselves or others and should make the

00:38:03.600 --> 00:38:09.180
highest sacrifices - including human sacrifices. Of
 course, such quotations also brought Nietzsche

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:15.930
close to fascism. And you know, Mussolini too, he

00:38:15.930 --> 00:38:20.790
really read Nietzsche and had huge monuments 
erected for Nietzsche. And this was because, initially,

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:25.470
he recognized no limits to this principle.
But, ladies and gentlemen,

00:38:25.470 --> 00:38:30.510
we're trying to look at it this in a non-ideological
 way, without immediately

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:37.440
pushing it in any direction. And if you do this
you must be honest and say that sometimes you do

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:44.550
sacrifice yourself for a higher purpose. It doesn't
 matter whether you feel good or bad about it,

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:48.930
nor how others feel. If necessary, we 
must make human sacrifices.

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:54.090
Just think of the former German Chancellor,
 the former Federal Chancellor Helmut

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:58.290
Schmidt. He got into a very difficult,

00:38:58.290 --> 00:39:06.180
you could say tragic situation. Because the
Baader-Meinhof group were being held in prison,

00:39:06.180 --> 00:39:11.970
some of whom had been accused of murder
 several times. Then the terrorists who

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:18.030
some of them accused of multiple murders.
Then the Baader-Meinhof terrorists who

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:25.530
were still outside hijacked a Lufthansa plane 
and also Hanns-Martin Schleyer, head of the

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:31.410
Confederation of German Industry, and said 
 they would release everyone provided

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:34.004
Ulrike Meinhof, Andreas Baader and the other
Jailed terrorists were released.

00:39:34.004 --> 00:39:37.493
And this meant that
 Helmut Schmidt and his cabinet faced

00:39:37.493 --> 00:39:41.936
a very difficult decision. And he said, 
no, we won't let them go, he had

00:39:41.936 --> 00:39:46.735
the Lufthansa plane stormed. And that's
 why Hanns Martin Schleyer was

00:39:46.735 --> 00:39:51.465
executed, so to speak, killed by the terrorists.
 And that's crazy, Schmidt even sat

00:39:51.465 --> 00:39:57.420
next to Frau Schleyer at the 
funeral. That was a really,

00:39:57.420 --> 00:40:03.420
really hard time for him. But he said that 
he would make that sacrifice again. So,

00:40:03.420 --> 00:40:10.050
as you can see here, he had a higher goal,
 a goal of the sort for the sake of which one

00:40:10.050 --> 00:40:14.820
does not hesitate to make human sacrifices. He 
had that goal. His goal was reasons of state. He

00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:20.820
said that things will not work unless the rule of law
convicts perpetrators and brings them to justice.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:25.710
let's set them free. Especially if they are murderers
 who might even kill again. He says:

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:31.530
let's set them free. Anyway, if they are murderers
 who might even kill again. Then he says

00:40:31.530 --> 00:40:36.300
no, that's not possible. The higher goal in 
this case is the rule of law. And our justice

00:40:36.300 --> 00:40:41.820
must be above this release
and he would basically, well, what did he mean

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:46.410
when he said "sacrifice again" Hanns Martin Schleyer? -
he didn't want to sacrifice him, of course,

00:40:46.410 --> 00:40:49.470
he deliberately risked this life and said he would do it
 again. And by the way he also wrote

00:40:49.470 --> 00:40:53.550
a note, a written document that he deposited

00:40:53.550 --> 00:40:59.220
saying that if he himself were kidnapped
 or Loki Schmidt, his wife, that they

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:03.660
must not be exchanged. He also deposited that.
 So he also made the sacrifice for himself,

00:41:03.660 --> 00:41:10.350
which he now asked for. Or he would have been
 willing to make that sacrifice. So you see,

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:16.350
you can also interpret it that way. Of course, 
Nietzsche not only extended his will to power

00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:21.880
to the individual but also said that 
it applies to large nations.

00:41:21.880 --> 00:41:26.530
Great peoples in history have
also lived out their will to power,

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:32.260
which, for Nietzsche, is not a bad thing.
 He says, for example: "Those big

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:35.225
hothouses for the strong, for the
 strongest kind of man" – 

00:41:35.225 --> 00:41:39.580
"the strongest kind of man", here we see this
 "strong and weak", which is why he is also

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:44.860
criticized a lot. "Those big hothouses for the strong
 and for the strongest kind of man that

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:50.830
has ever existed, the aristocratic polities
 in the manner of Rome and Venice

00:41:50.830 --> 00:41:59.620
understood freedom exactly in the sense in which I
 understand the word freedom: as something that one

00:41:59.620 --> 00:42:08.890
has and that one does not have , that one wants, that
 one conquers." So he has Rome in mind, ancient

00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:16.420
Rome and Venice. These were communities that
 used their freedom and their self-development

00:42:16.420 --> 00:42:23.740
to conquer, to keep and to gain what they didn’t yet have

00:42:23.740 --> 00:42:28.390
That's why Mussolini found Nietzsche 
so great, because he also

00:42:28.390 --> 00:42:34.060
wanted to revive ancient Rome and its old splendor.
 But we'll see in a moment that Nietzsche wasn't

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:38.650
really interested in that. He also celebrates
 Venice. And if you then read on,

00:42:38.650 --> 00:42:44.860
he says, the northern Italian cities also 
developed the will to power in a great way. And

00:42:44.860 --> 00:42:49.000
I'll show you why right now. By the way, 
he says: In the Renaissance, at that time,

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:55.300
when Venice and later Florence and those
northern Italian cities had this crazy power,

00:42:55.300 --> 00:43:01.420
mankind took an enormous step forward.
 He even claims that the Europeans of today

00:43:01.420 --> 00:43:10.630
“remain at a level below the
Europeans of the Renaissance in terms of their values".

00:43:10.630 --> 00:43:16.030
Well, they did something great
in the Renaissance. And he wasn't

00:43:16.030 --> 00:43:23.030
concerned with the fact that Venice and its merchant fleet
 powerfully controlled the entire Mediterranean region,

00:43:23.030 --> 00:43:26.418
nor the Borgia ruling in Renaissance Italy

00:43:26.418 --> 00:43:34.063
but essentially with the fact that in the
 Renaissance an enormous act of human self-liberation 

00:43:34.063 --> 00:43:40.390
had taken place. Science flourished for the first time.

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:46.180
Doctors were allowed to dissect corpses. And for 
the first time human beings were freed from

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:53.140
millennia-old medieval thinking. You
 know, the artists, for example

00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:58.900
Leonardo da Vinci or Michelangelo,
 were also scientists. They

00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:06.760
had a completely free, enlightened way of thinking. And 
for the first time perspective was also painted. The

00:44:06.760 --> 00:44:12.190
whole Middle Ages was only two-dimensional.
 You could see the haloes there,

00:44:12.190 --> 00:44:14.920
but they had no depth. That's when
 it started, when perspective was introduced.

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:18.460
Then all the sciences blossomed.
 This inspired Nietzsche.

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:25.120
Or Brunelleschi, who built his
 huge dome. In the Renaissance

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:28.870
people liberated themselves. And the artists,
by the way, signed their works themselves.

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:34.870
Suddenly, beautiful naked bodies were 
painted. It was much more Dionysian,

00:44:34.870 --> 00:44:39.280
he says, the Renaissance. Because nudity was
 completely forbidden in the Middle Ages.

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:43.930
And then of course, how should I put it,
 they did something exciting. The

00:44:43.930 --> 00:44:48.460
Renaissance artists simply said, yes, 
we paint ancient things like that,

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:54.370
Botticelli's Venus, and of course such an ancient
 goddess, Venus, has to be naked. And

00:44:54.370 --> 00:45:00.130
he just liked this awakening.
He writes, for example, that:

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:07.476
"the Italian Renaissance contained the liberation 
of thought, the disregard for authority,

00:45:07.476 --> 00:45:11.770
 enthusiasm for science, the unleashing of 
the individual, a fervor of veracity."

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:18.580
enthusiasm for science, unleashing 
the individual, a fervor of veracity."

00:45:18.580 --> 00:45:24.610
So you see, for Nietzsche, "will to power" 
doesn't necessarily mean huge conquests,

00:45:24.610 --> 00:45:30.190
it's more about intellectual development,
 artistic development.

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:41.400
And that brings us to our initial question
 and our last big topic. How does one

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:47.670
do that? How can we develop ourselves?
What is this overman that

00:45:47.670 --> 00:45:55.140
he recommends to us? "The gods are dead, now
 we want the superman to live." But who or

00:45:55.140 --> 00:46:02.790
what is this overman? And can those of us
gathered here also become “overmen”?

00:46:02.790 --> 00:46:09.060
Let's look at that now; finally.
"The overman", says Nietzsche,

00:46:09.060 --> 00:46:17.130
""is close to my heart, that is my first
 and only thing and not the human being:

00:46:17.130 --> 00:46:25.950
not ‘my neighbour’, not the poorest, not the
most suffering, not the ‘best’." And it's

00:46:25.950 --> 00:46:31.500
interesting that he says that the overrman is
important to him and not the human being. And that

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:37.530
this overman is something different from his “neighbour”. 
That’s actually clear, and not the poorest,

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:42.750
and not the most suffering either, that's what
 we already know from Nietzsche, his,

00:46:42.750 --> 00:46:48.870
how should I put it, negative assessment of 
Christian heroes, that is, the hero on the cross,

00:46:48.870 --> 00:46:56.760
Jesus on the cross is the most suffering or the
 poorest. How should I say? The overman

00:46:56.760 --> 00:47:02.670
of religions is the ascetic, like Buddha, 
who withdraws, or just like Jesus, or

00:47:02.670 --> 00:47:07.530
also the Franciscans and other mendicant orders,
 who owned nothing but a rope around their stomachs

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:14.520
and a few robes. There was this ideal of the 
denial of all property, of everything belonging

00:47:14.520 --> 00:47:18.900
to this world. And this Nietzsche rejected.
That's not the overman. The overman is

00:47:18.900 --> 00:47:22.830
not the ascetic. You know that Nietzsche had a lot in 
common with Schopenhauer, and Schopenhauer says

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:28.740
you have to be as ascetic as possible. Nietzsche
 says: no, no, the overman

00:47:28.740 --> 00:47:34.830
is not the one who suffers, not the poorest, not
one’s needy “neighbour”, and - now it's getting exciting -

00:47:34.830 --> 00:47:40.800
he is not even “the best”. Why not the best? 
based on what we know from Nietzsche so far,

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:48.850
one would think that the overman is the strongest,
ergo also the best. But that is not what Nietzsche 

00:47:48.850 --> 00:47:55.240
means. It's not about the
 overman’s being

00:47:55.240 --> 00:48:01.030
 a capitalist who is incredibly successful
 in business, or a politician, or Alexander

00:48:01.030 --> 00:48:05.350
the Great who conquers a huge empire. He
does not claim that “the best man” is

00:48:05.350 --> 00:48:13.270
is the overman. He says: "The higher nature
 of the great man lies in his incommunicability" – 

00:48:13.270 --> 00:48:20.620
this is interesting – “in his incommunicability,
 not in the fact that the effects he produced

00:48:20.620 --> 00:48:27.460
may have shaken the globe." So if the higher 
nature of the great man leads to the overman,

00:48:27.460 --> 00:48:32.260
this is not because he 
 he has a huge impact and shakes the whole world,

00:48:32.260 --> 00:48:39.070
but rather in his incommunicability. What does he
 mean by someone being “incommunicable”?

00:48:39.070 --> 00:48:44.050
And why should that be considered a good quality: 
that he cannot make himself understood?

00:48:45.310 --> 00:48:50.290
But Nietzsche explains this very well. 
He says it's ultimately about finding

00:48:50.290 --> 00:49:00.160
our own inner higher idea, that is, our
 innermost creative potential, our

00:49:00.160 --> 00:49:04.600
innermost higher being. If we find that, then
 we have to live it out. And then it doesn't matter

00:49:04.600 --> 00:49:09.970
whether we are understood and can communicate.
 We have to assert this idea all the same. And he writes,

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:14.830
many great writers and also painters,
 they had something like that,

00:49:14.830 --> 00:49:21.340
they did their thing, regardless of whether they
 were understood. For example – this is

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:26.320
not by Nietzsche himself but I think
 the example is quite good – Van Gogh,

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:34.450
Van Gogh was destitute all 
his life. And everyone just

00:49:34.450 --> 00:49:39.700
shook their heads at his pictures. Nobody
 understood him because he painted

00:49:39.700 --> 00:49:48.610
in a completely new way. He is one of the founding
 fathers of Expressionism. When he saw a cornfield

00:49:48.610 --> 00:49:54.040
with ravens overhead and a storm came
 up, he painted it. It's totally spooky.

00:49:54.040 --> 00:49:59.050
He was able to bring his mood, 
his way of perceiving reality,

00:49:59.050 --> 00:50:03.270
directly onto the canvas and this meant the end 
of “idyllic” painting. And everyone said

00:50:03.270 --> 00:50:08.280
“I’m not hanging stuff like that on my wall”.
 So he encountered huge obstacles. No one

00:50:08.280 --> 00:50:14.730
understood this new kind of fervent 
painting at all. Or the sunflowers.

00:50:15.540 --> 00:50:20.310
There you can see these sunflowers - beautiful
 and great and powerful, vital. But you can

00:50:20.310 --> 00:50:25.680
already see a few wilted leaves and you can see
 this whole metamorphosis, can understand the

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:29.970
mortality and suffering of this flower. 
This message is also in there. But that was

00:50:29.970 --> 00:50:35.880
not understood at all at the time, this new realism on 
the one hand, and on the other hand this direct way

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:39.540
of capturing his feelings just with these 
dashed lines that he painted five times over.

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:46.860
And you know Van Gogh died destitute.
 And today these paintings

00:50:46.860 --> 00:50:52.650
are the most expensive paintings in the whole world.
Nothing else attracts such prices and Nietzsche would

00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:56.130
say: “Sure! It shouldn't matter if we

00:50:56.130 --> 00:51:02.130
get no recognition from others just now.
 And it shouldn't matter if one day

00:51:02.130 --> 00:51:07.290
we somehow become ridiculously rich
 through what we do.” So it is

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:13.170
by no means the case that today he would say that 
the “overmen” are the managers and the bankers.

00:51:13.170 --> 00:51:17.640
He would consider them anything but overmen. He would
 say: "Look at these superfluous ones!

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:23.880
They acquire riches and become poorer with it
 - these impotent ones." You notice, then, what a

00:51:23.880 --> 00:51:29.640
constantly shifting description he ultimately gives 
of his “overman”. And he also adds:

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:37.230
"Passionate people think little of what others
 think. Their condition elevates them

00:51:37.230 --> 00:51:43.170
above vanity." That is also important to him. 
Of course, a lot of this comes from his own life.

00:51:43.170 --> 00:51:50.670
Because he himself, you know, was
 destitute, so to speak. Nobody

00:51:50.670 --> 00:51:55.350
read Nietzsche back then. They all
 thought he was a nutcase. 

00:51:55.350 --> 00:51:59.490
Publishers stopped accepting his books
And, in desperation, he had to

00:51:59.490 --> 00:52:01.710
accepting his books. Then, in desperation, he had to

00:52:01.710 --> 00:52:07.500
print them himself. It was not until
 towards the end of his life that

00:52:07.500 --> 00:52:12.750
his work actually fell
on more fertile ground.

00:52:12.750 --> 00:52:19.620
But he was ill already, so never experienced 
it himself. But that's just, so to speak,

00:52:19.620 --> 00:52:27.570
a sidelight. And he just says: "Everyone
 has their good day, on which

00:52:27.570 --> 00:52:33.840
they find a higher self." So that's what it’s all about
for him: that we find our higher self. He says

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:38.820
everyone has moments like that. "For example, a painter
 should be appraised and honored for the highest vision that

00:52:38.820 --> 00:52:42.900
he was able to see and depict." 
And not whether 

00:52:42.900 --> 00:52:50.250
he was successful or anything. It
 was important to Nietzsche that we

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:58.020
realize our innermost creative potential. And to be
 completely free and to enter new spaces. This is, of course,

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:03.060
difficult to understand at first and also obscure.
 In addition, he describes the overman in his

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:08.550
book Zarathustra. And Zarathustra is not
 a conventional philosophical work.

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:15.690
It is a poetic work where he describes his
 great prophet Zarathustra. At the beginning

00:53:15.690 --> 00:53:22.350
of the book Zarathustra descends from the mountains
 and tells the world what it should look like.

00:53:22.350 --> 00:53:28.830
So he created his own prophet. And this
 Zarathustra then tells us how the path

00:53:28.830 --> 00:53:35.640
to the new human being should go. And
 it can be argued that he is maybe

00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:39.780
 more concerned here with artists, geniuses,
philosophers etc. and not with ordinary folk

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:46.020
philosophers, etc. but I maintain that but I maintain that he also 
has a few other quotes which show that

00:53:46.020 --> 00:53:50.520
this is not just about geniuses. So here 
he also writes: "Everyone has their good

00:53:50.520 --> 00:53:55.830
day" and what I also find interesting, is that 
in Zarathustra he gives us a hint, that I want to

00:53:55.830 --> 00:54:02.790
end by passing on to you, that, on the way to the
overman, we have to go through three transformations.

00:54:02.790 --> 00:54:08.880
I'll give you that hint now. "I shall name you three 
transformations of the spirit”

00:54:08.880 --> 00:54:16.260
spirit." So you have to go through three transformations
 of the spirit: "How the spirit becomes

00:54:16.260 --> 00:54:22.290
a camel, and the camel becomes a lion, and finally the lion
 becomes a child." A very famous parable by Nietzsche.

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:31.290
So the spirit first becomes a camel, then the 
camel becomes a lion, and finally the lion

00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:39.390
becomes a child. Exciting - why the child? By the camel
 he means – oh and by the way, this parable

00:54:39.390 --> 00:54:44.790
clearly refers to two things at once: our
development as individuals and the history of mankind

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:51.000
i.e. the social moment – 
Individually, the phase of the camel,

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:58.590
which we all go through, is that of our training
 period, when we learn, when our parents,

00:54:58.590 --> 00:55:04.890
our mentors, our teachers, place more and more 
burdens on us. So the phase of the camel,

00:55:04.890 --> 00:55:11.760
he says, is the patient, tolerant phase that we all
 go through. We take on more and more knowledge,

00:55:11.760 --> 00:55:19.440
more and more morals, more and more laws, more 
and more patterns of behavior, etiquette, rules, etc.

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:24.960
This is the camel's phase in our youth. 
Then comes the phase of the lion.

00:55:24.960 --> 00:55:31.050
In the phase of the lion we shred
 all this, we tear it up. We

00:55:31.050 --> 00:55:35.700
free ourselves from the old dramas, 
from the old traditions, from

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:40.530
everything our parents told us. That's
 the phase of the lion. The lion tears

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:46.440
everything with his teeth. It's often interpreted
 like this. For many, puberty is the time when you

00:55:46.440 --> 00:55:52.005
liberate yourself. For some, however, it occurs
 much later, in adulthood. For others, it

00:55:52.005 --> 00:55:55.200
really doesn't come until the midlife crisis. 
This doesn’t really matter. Let's leave that aside.

00:55:55.200 --> 00:56:04.440
In terms of mankind as a whole he means: The phase of 
The lion is this phase where mankind.... Sorry - 

00:56:04.440 --> 00:56:12.720
let's start with the camel. In the beginning we are all
believers. We live, so to speak, in humility and are

00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:19.200
at ease in the religious worldview. He calls this whole
 long phase the phase of the camel. But then,

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:26.310
  how should I put it, the lion – and Nietzsche 
didn't elaborate further but he could

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:30.240
mean the Enlightenment, where we 
actually tear everything apart

00:56:30.240 --> 00:56:33.780
and question it. So the beginning of 
science and everything. But,

00:56:33.780 --> 00:56:37.590
he says, we mustn't stop there. 
We mustn't stop there with the

00:56:37.590 --> 00:56:44.520
phase of the lion: it's not enough if we just
liberate ourselves, be it as individuals or as a species,

00:56:44.520 --> 00:56:53.790
that is, from the point of view of the social situation.
He then says: now the transformation into

00:56:53.790 --> 00:57:01.560
the playing child must come. And then he asks
 an interesting question. He poses it in that slightly,

00:57:01.560 --> 00:57:06.990
how should I put it, emotive and histrionic tone
that he adopts in Zarathustra, who is speaking to his disciples

00:57:06.990 --> 00:57:11.730
and to his brothers. By the way, this prophet 
Zarathustra is a great book, so it's

00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:18.660
fun to read it and he says: "But tell me,
 my brothers, what can the child

00:57:18.660 --> 00:57:24.870
do that the lion couldn't do either? I mean, so far 
it's been up to us. Yes, of course. The first phase

00:57:24.870 --> 00:57:31.860
is the camel, then the lion, but why is this last great
 transformation a transformation into a

00:57:31.860 --> 00:57:36.960
playing child? With Nietzsche, one actually
 expects that on the way to the overman the

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:43.470
last transformation will be into a huge monster
 or an all-powerful dragon or the devil knows what.

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:49.950
No, for the last transformation, he asks: "What can 
the child do that the lion couldn't do?" And

00:57:49.950 --> 00:57:57.570
he replies: "Create new values, 
the lion can't do that either: but

00:57:57.570 --> 00:58:03.090
create freedom for yourself to create new things, that's what the
 power of the lion is capable of". So the lion can only make room

00:58:03.090 --> 00:58:09.960
for us to create something new. But he doesn't 
set any new values yet. He doesn't create that

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:17.640
yet. And Nietzsche also says that most people at some  
point in their lives succeed in freeing themselves

00:58:17.640 --> 00:58:20.730
from their upbringing, their origins,

00:58:20.730 --> 00:58:27.450
from all these things and all possible influences.
But the decisive thing is now: What do we do

00:58:27.450 --> 00:58:33.660
with our freedom? what do we do with it? And 
he says, sometimes – he wasn't very sociable,

00:58:33.660 --> 00:58:38.550
Nietzsche, he tended to live alone –
sometimes friends visited him and, as

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:44.460
he writes very interestingly, everyone always 
told him what they had emancipated and freed

00:58:44.460 --> 00:58:49.440
themselves from, and what they’d finally left
 behind them. But he was actually more interested

00:58:49.440 --> 00:58:55.710
in what they’d become free for! Not in what they’d freed
 themselves from, but why, how they were now

00:58:55.710 --> 00:59:02.490
fulfilling their freedom. And he writes: "I want
 to hear your ruling thoughts and not

00:59:02.490 --> 00:59:10.620
that you have escaped a yoke. Free from what?
What does that matter to Zarathustra! Brightly

00:59:10.620 --> 00:59:19.140
your eyes should tell me, free for what?" And that's 
what it's all about, that in this phase of life we find out

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:29.700
what our own Dionysian creative potential might 
fit us to do, so to speak. And this is why the transformation

00:59:29.700 --> 00:59:34.740
into the child is important, into the
 playing child. Now we can understand why.

00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:41.840
Because in this phase after the liberation we 
basically have to start from scratch. Basically ,

00:59:41.840 --> 00:59:49.970
we have to go back into the world with 
an open mind. And then he says: "The

00:59:49.970 --> 00:59:57.170
child is innocence and forgetting, a new beginning, 
a game, a wheel rolling out of itself, a first movement,

00:59:57.170 --> 01:00:10.160
a holy yes-saying. Yes to the game, to the game 
of creation." So, he's saying here, the child is

01:00:10.160 --> 01:00:16.340
innocent, it is not bearing any burdens. And the 
child has this holy ability to say yes to creativity.

01:00:16.340 --> 01:00:21.860
You know how we could get into it 
as children and play passionately. We

01:00:21.860 --> 01:00:27.170
have to acquire this again. And he doesn't just mean
 the really great artists. But, of course, there

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:33.440
is also a quote from Picasso, I think, that fits
 very well at this point. Even Picasso

01:00:33.440 --> 01:00:41.510
had the big problem of having left behind, 
how should I put it, the whole tradition

01:00:41.510 --> 01:00:47.600
through his abstract painting. For 2000 years
 people painted things as copies and suddenly he

01:00:47.600 --> 01:00:51.920
painted abstractly. And he didn't dare to do 
that right away. And Picasso once said:

01:00:51.920 --> 01:00:58.160
"It takes a long time to become young."
 I think that's a nice quote,

01:00:58.160 --> 01:01:03.710
which basically means exactly what Nietzsche
 said. It takes a long, long time

01:01:03.710 --> 01:01:10.280
to grow young. And for him, being young 
simply meant seeing things the way

01:01:10.280 --> 01:01:16.010
he had seen them before developing prejudices. 
And then he got into this abstraction. And that's

01:01:16.010 --> 01:01:23.060
what Nietzsche is all about. Hence this third transformation
 into the playing child. And some say that, by the

01:01:23.060 --> 01:01:27.530
overman, he meant individual outstanding
 personalities who come along from time to time.

01:01:27.530 --> 01:01:31.310
But I think it's permissible to consider
whether that might not also apply to us all.

01:01:31.310 --> 01:01:38.630
Nietzsche has, for example, the aphorism: "Maturity.-

01:01:38.630 --> 01:01:44.030
For a man means having rediscovered the seriousness 
that one had as a child when playing."

01:01:44.030 --> 01:01:47.510
Maturity for a man means 
having rediscovered the seriousness that

01:01:47.510 --> 01:01:49.580
one had as a child when playing."

01:01:49.580 --> 01:01:54.920
The same goes for women, of course. Maturity 
for a woman would also mean finding the

01:01:54.920 --> 01:02:00.140
seriousness again, to passionately get
 involved in such a game, to immerse

01:02:00.140 --> 01:02:07.100
oneself in it, to lose oneself in it. These are the things 
Nietzsche cares about. These are the steps on the way to the

01:02:07.100 --> 01:02:12.170
higher self. Basically, what I'm saying, 
what I'm claiming, is that this “overman” idea is one

01:02:12.170 --> 01:02:19.280
big self-actualization story, “coming-out” 
story that he's telling us. Good,

01:02:19.280 --> 01:02:24.470
but there is a big obstacle when
 we now think about whether this

01:02:24.470 --> 01:02:31.760
would also be an option for us. Nietzsche writes himself
about this great obstacle, namely time. He says:

01:02:31.760 --> 01:02:38.840
"Everyone still falls today, as they have at all times,
Into one or another of two classes, namely:

01:02:38.840 --> 01:02:46.820
 into slaves and free people. Because whoever
 does not have two thirds of his day for himself

01:02:46.820 --> 01:02:53.780
is a slave, whatever else he is: 
statesman, merchant, civil servant, scholar."

01:02:53.780 --> 01:02:58.280
So if you don't have at least two-thirds of your
 day to yourself, you're a slave, regardless

01:02:58.280 --> 01:03:03.800
of whether you're a minister or a businessman, 
you're a slave. And I often think to myself,

01:03:03.800 --> 01:03:05.990
ladies and gentlemen, when I come 
home in the evening and I'm

01:03:05.990 --> 01:03:10.850
exhausted after an 8-hour day, that I  
have no more creative thoughts in me. You turn the

01:03:10.850 --> 01:03:16.970
TV on and off. And what Nietzsche is saying is that
this free time is almost a basic human right we should demand

01:03:16.970 --> 01:03:22.280
You really should have two-thirds of the 
day to yourself. Only then can we tap our

01:03:22.280 --> 01:03:27.590
full potential and pass it on to society. 
That would mean, let's assume we get

01:03:27.590 --> 01:03:37.070
up at 9, well let's say 8 a.m., and say 
from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., that would be

01:03:37.070 --> 01:03:41.960
the part of the day where we’re really
fit and awake. And two thirds of that, that

01:03:41.960 --> 01:03:46.670
would now be twelve hours. That would be two 
thirds, if I calculate it correctly, we basically need,

01:03:46.670 --> 01:03:49.250
So we should really only

01:03:49.250 --> 01:03:59.016
work four hours a day. What he's 
proposing here is the four-hour work-day.

01:03:59.016 --> 01:04:04.240
…For example - yes, absolutely…
The circle will close even more now,

01:04:04.240 --> 01:04:10.330
because I would like to bring things to a close, so to
 speak. So Nietzsche says, I summarize:

01:04:10.330 --> 01:04:16.810
God is dead. The “new idols”, capitalism, socialism, 
nationalism, racism, are not things we should worship.

01:04:16.810 --> 01:04:23.290
we don't worship. That would be wrong. Only 
the small minds do that, who are only capable

01:04:23.290 --> 01:04:30.250
of developing false feelings instead of
 developing their own lives themselves. And

01:04:30.250 --> 01:04:35.800
Rather, we must try to develop ourselves determinedly 
after the death of God, and to unfold

01:04:35.800 --> 01:04:41.560
our higher inner being. And here Nietzsche 
gives us two more hints. On the

01:04:41.560 --> 01:04:48.280
one hand, it is important that we become more Dionysian
 again, that is, that we live with relish, that

01:04:48.280 --> 01:04:53.500
we let the Dionysian principle that has been suppressed
 for centuries, the creative Dionysian, have

01:04:54.460 --> 01:04:59.230
its say again. Although I have to tell you: 
He also says that there are some people

01:04:59.230 --> 01:05:04.240
who have more Dionysian qualities and some 
who have more Apollonian ones. I myself

01:05:04.240 --> 01:05:09.490
ran a film school for years and the Dionysians – 
that is, my students training to be film directors – 

01:05:10.270 --> 01:05:14.080
were very difficult people. Because they
draw from their innermost being and then they are

01:05:14.080 --> 01:05:19.690
no longer capable of self-criticism. Because that
 comes from a source that is beyond criticism.

01:05:19.690 --> 01:05:24.310
With the Apollonians, who think logically,
you can discuss things with them. It's about

01:05:24.310 --> 01:05:29.170
arguments. The Dionysians are difficult. 
But that's just a digression now. And

01:05:29.170 --> 01:05:33.130
of course they have to be like that, the creative
 people, you have to say that again. They have

01:05:33.130 --> 01:05:38.590
to rely on what they have recognized
 as innermost, highest. But Nietzsche

01:05:38.590 --> 01:05:43.630
actually advises us to consider, on our way in life,
 our Dionysian principle once again,

01:05:43.630 --> 01:05:48.670
to respect our intuition and secondly – which
I actually find quite beautiful –

01:05:48.670 --> 01:05:53.440
this image, this thought that 
we should perhaps try to

01:05:53.440 --> 01:05:58.480
become a playing child again. And so I would
 like to close with a quote from Nietzsche:

01:05:58.480 --> 01:06:04.660
"One must still have chaos in oneself in order to be able
 to give birth to a dancing star." Thank you very much.

